      "articleId": "aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXNhaGkuY29tL2FydGljbGVzL0FTVkJIMVFYVFZCSFVIQkkwMVBNLmh0bWw=",
      "headline": "反ワクチンの旗振るトランプ政権閣僚　米ではしか急増、日本に影響も：朝日新聞",
      "headlineTranslated": "Trump administration cabinet members waving the anti-vaccine flag; measles surge in the United States, Japan also affected — Asahi Shimbun",
      "summary": "Measles infections in the United States exceed 4,000 as of October 2026, the largest surge since elimination was declared in 2000. The outbreak correlates with declining preschool MMR vaccination coverage; 95% is the threshold to halt transmission, yet the 2025–26 kindergarten-entry MMR rate fell to 92.4% from 95.2% in 2019–20, with states below 90% increasing from 3 to 18. CDC data indicate about 95% of confirmed cases in 2025–26 occurred in unvaccinated individuals or those with unknown vaccination status. The WHO is poised to re‑evaluate elimination status this autumn, potentially overturning it. Commentary from Penn Medicine Lancaster General Hospital cites vaccine hesitancy amid public-health policy debates during the Trump era, with attribution to anti‑vaccine advocates such as Robert F. Kennedy Jr. The article also notes that Japan is affected amid global travel.",
      "summaryOriginal": "Measles infections in the United States exceed 4,000 as of October 2026, the largest surge since elimination was declared in 2000. The outbreak correlates with declining preschool MMR vaccination coverage; 95% is the threshold to halt transmission, yet the 2025–26 kindergarten-entry MMR rate fell to 92.4% from 95.2% in 2019–20, with states below 90% increasing from 3 to 18. CDC data indicate about 95% of confirmed cases in 2025–26 occurred in unvaccinated individuals or those with unknown vaccination status. The WHO is poised to re‑evaluate elimination status this autumn, potentially overturning it. Commentary from Penn Medicine Lancaster General Hospital cites vaccine hesitancy amid public-health policy debates during the Trump era, with attribution to anti‑vaccine advocates such as Robert F. Kennedy Jr. The article also notes that Japan is affected amid global travel.",
